+## `ws-r186-join-sheet-primary-buttons-pinned-with-sticky-not-trimmed` (2026-09-14, WS-R186)
+
+**Decision.** `src/room/room.css`'s `.room-join .room-actions` (scoped to the join sheet only, never the shared `.room-actions` class the account page, cap card and gone screen also use) gets `position: sticky; bottom: 0` plus a background/blur/border matching `.room-composer`'s own already-shipped pattern, rather than shrinking the vertical spacing or line-height of the disclosure card, the about link, the age line, or the memory explanation (three kept items, three sentences of fine print) that precede it.
+
+**Why.** `evals/rehearsal/visitor-first-minute.mjs`'s own fold check measured the real, signed-in join sheet at 390x844: the primary "Yes, remember me"/"Not now" buttons rendered with their own bottom edge at 937-993px against an 844px viewport — 93-149px past the fold, meaning a real visitor's phone would need to scroll to find them, the exact defect this workstream's brief law 4 names ("nothing below the fold that must be tapped"). This file's own law (and CLAUDE.md's) says copy stays as it is unless a line fails the read-aloud test — none of the preceding content failed that test, so shortening it was never a candidate fix. Trimming only the SPACING between blocks (margins/gaps, never the text itself) was considered and rejected as insufficient without being built and measured: the actual per-block margins available to cut (`--space-block`/`--space-item`/`--space-tight`, `room.css`'s own token comments) sum to roughly 40-50px of recoverable space across every block on this screen, nowhere near the ~150px gap, because the real cost is the TEXT's own line-height (roughly 15-20 lines of body/small copy at `--leading-body: 1.6`), not the gaps between paragraphs — see `context/rejected.md#ws-r186-margin-trimming-alone-cannot-close-a-150px-fold-overflow` for the arithmetic this reasoning rests on. Sticky positioning was verified EMPIRICALLY, not assumed from the CSS spec, before it was applied to the real component: a throwaway fixture (a 1200px div followed by a `position: sticky; bottom: 0` footer) read the footer's own `getBoundingClientRect()` at `scrollTop: 0` in the real Chromium this repo already uses, and its bottom edge read exactly at the viewport's own bottom (844px) on the FIRST paint, never requiring a scroll to "catch" it first — settling, before touching product code, the one part of sticky's behavior that was not obvious from reading the spec alone.
+
+**Reversal condition.** If a future change to the join sheet's own copy (a shorter disclosure card, say) closes the gap on its own, this override becomes a no-op and can be deleted once `evals/rehearsal/visitor-first-minute.mjs`'s own fold check still passes without it — never removed speculatively without re-running that check. If the "no means" note that follows `.room-actions` in the DOM (WS-R27's own "stated where 'no' is chosen") is ever found to be visually obscured by the pinned row during a real scroll-through (not observed in this session's own layout-gate screenshots, `node scripts/check-layout.mjs --only room`, `evals/room-browser/shots`), the fix is to give the note its own `scroll-margin-bottom` equal to the pinned row's height, never to unpin the row again.
