+## `ws-r186-margin-trimming-alone-cannot-close-a-150px-fold-overflow` (2026-09-14, WS-R186)
+
+**Reasoned about, not built or measured — named as reasoning rather than presented as a measurement, `CLAUDE.md`'s own "prefer measuring to reasoning, and when you cannot measure something, say so" restated for a path not taken.** Before choosing the sticky-footer fix (`context/decisions.md#ws-r186-join-sheet-primary-buttons-pinned-with-sticky-not-trimmed`), considered tightening the join sheet's own vertical spacing tokens instead — never touching the copy itself, only the gaps between blocks. `room.css`'s own token comments give every candidate a number: h3's own `margin: var(--space-block) 0 var(--space-tight)` (24px top, 8px bottom, one heading on this screen); `.room-lede`/`.room-fine`'s shared `margin: 0 0 var(--space-item)` (16px, roughly four occurrences on this screen — the join lede, the age line's own "why" sentence, the memory lede, the "only/private/undo" fine print); `.room-check`'s `margin-bottom: var(--space-item)` (16px); `.room-keeps`'s `margin: 0 0 var(--space-item)` (16px) plus `--space-hair` (4px) between its own three list items; `.room-note`'s `margin: var(--space-item) 0 0` (16px). Trimming each of these to the next-smaller token in this repo's own scale (`--space-item` 16px down to `--space-row` 12px, say) sums to roughly 40-50px of recoverable space across the whole screen — a real, plausible tenth of the ~150px the join sheet's own primary buttons overran the fold by (937-993px against an 844px viewport, `context/measurements.md#ws-r186-join-sheet-actions-past-the-fold-before-the-pin`). The remaining ~100px was never going to come from spacing: `room.css`'s own body/small text sits at `--leading-body: 1.6`, and this screen carries roughly 15-20 lines of real, meaningful copy across the disclosure card, the about link, the join lede, the age line, the memory lede, three kept items and three sentences of fine print — line-height, not inter-block margin, is the dominant cost, and none of that text failed the read-aloud test this file's own law reserves for a copy change. Abandoned in favor of pinning the actions row instead, which needs no copy or spacing change at all and was verified empirically before being applied (that decision's own header).
+
+## `ws-r186-room-forget-rate-limited-to-3-per-minute-per-identity-broke-a-naive-reset-between-runs`
+
+**Tried.** `evals/rehearsal/visitor-first-minute.mjs`'s own n=3-per-locale timed measurement needed each of the three cold contexts to be a genuinely fresh follower at the SERVER layer too, not only a fresh browser `localStorage` (this file's own header explains why — a returning follower's second device skips the join sheet entirely). The obvious approach was calling the real `POST /api/room {op:"forget"}` door after EVERY run and EVERY structural check that joins (three timed runs, the fold/keyboard/install-prompt check) — one call per boundary, four total for one locale.
+
+**What specifically broke.** `api/room.js`'s own in-memory rate limiter (`allow(authUserId, \`room_${op}_user\`, 3)`, `api/_ratelimit.js`, a 60-second sliding window) refuses a FOURTH `forget` for the same identity inside one minute with `429 slow_down` — a real product ceiling (three forgets/exports a minute is already generous for a human, and exists to stop a script draining the door), not a fixture gap. The suite's own fourth call, from a dedicated `checkJoinSheetAndKeyboardSafety` function called right after the three timed runs' own two inter-run forgets plus one more before it, hit this exactly.
+
+**Fix.** The fold check, the keyboard-safety check, and the install-prompt negative control were merged into ONE walk (`checkJoinSheetKeyboardAndInstallPrompt`) sharing a single join — the install-prompt control's own "visit 2" is a second navigation in the SAME context rather than a second join, so it needs no forget of its own. Total forgets per locale: exactly 3 (between run 1 and 2, between run 2 and 3, and once more before the merged structural/install check), inside the door's own 3/minute ceiling with nothing to spare — the paused-Room and never-published negative controls that follow reuse the same identity's now-intentionally-left-behind follower row, which their own predicates (`published_at`/`paused_at`) never consult anyway.
+
+**The rule.** A suite that resets follower state between runs through the product's own real "forget me" door, rather than reaching into fixture arrays by hand, gets the product's own real rate limits along with the realism — worth knowing before designing how many resets a walk needs, not after hitting a `429` mid-run. The next suite that wants more than 3 server-side resets for one identity inside a minute either spreads them further apart, uses a second fixture-recognized identity (today only `USER_A`/`USER_B` and the two owner tokens exist, `evals/rehearsal/stubs/auth-with-fake-user.mjs`'s own fixed `KNOWN` map), or accepts the ceiling as the real, product-correct behavior it is.
